# Howdy Mail

`howdy_mail` is an optional package for sending email from a Howdy app. It
gives you:

- a builder for messages, written with [smail](https://hexdocs.pm/smail)
  templates or plain HTML and text
- checks that run before anything is sent, so user input cannot add
  headers
- [adapters](/docs/mail/adapters) for SMTP, Resend and SendGrid, and a way
  to write your own
- a [development outbox and previews](/docs/mail/development) that keep
  mail instead of sending it, and show it in the admin area

[Howdy Auth](/docs/auth/overview) builds its ready-made emails on it; see
[Sending the email](/docs/auth/signing-in).

## Install

Add the package to `gleam.toml`, next to `howdy` and `smail`:

```toml
howdy_mail = { git = "https://github.com/howdy-gleam/howdy.git", ref = "v1.0.0", path = "mail" }
smail = ">= 2.0.1 and < 3.0.0"
```

## Writing a message

A message starts empty and is built up with a pipeline:

```gleam
import howdy/mail
import smail/attribute
import smail/email
import smail/html

pub fn welcome(user: User) -> mail.Message {
  mail.message()
  |> mail.to([mail.named(user.name, user.email)])
  |> mail.subject("Welcome to Acme")
  |> mail.template(
    email.html([], [
      email.head([], []),
      email.body([], [
        email.preview("Your account is ready"),
        email.container([], [
          email.paragraph([], [html.text("Hi " <> user.name <> ",")]),
          email.button([attribute.href("https://acme.test/start")], [
            html.text("Get started"),
          ]),
        ]),
      ]),
    ]),
  )
  |> mail.tag("onboarding.welcome")
}
```

Keep functions like `welcome` apart from the code that sends them. The
[previews](/docs/mail/development) in the admin area call them with sample
data.

### The body

- `mail.template(message, element)` renders a smail element to HTML, and
  to plain text for mail clients that do not show HTML.
- `mail.text(message, text)` sets the plain text yourself, and wins over
  the text `template` made.
- `mail.html(message, html)` takes HTML as it is, without smail.

Write the text with `mail.text` when it carries a link that matters. smail
can run the block after an `email.button` into the button's URL, which
breaks the link for anyone reading the text version. The admin's previews
show the text, so you can check.

### Addresses

- `mail.address("ada@example.com")` is an address on its own.
- `mail.named("Ada Lovelace", "ada@example.com")` shows the name too, as
  `Ada Lovelace <ada@example.com>`.
- `mail.parse_address("Ada <ada@example.com>")` reads one a person wrote,
  and returns `Error(Nil)` when it is not an address.

Addresses must be plain ASCII `local@domain` mailboxes. Addresses with other
characters need a feature not every server offers, so they are refused for
now.

### Everything else

- `mail.from(message, address)` sends from this address instead of the
  mailer's default.
- `mail.to`, `mail.cc` and `mail.bcc` take a list of addresses. `Bcc`
  recipients are hidden from the others.
- `mail.reply_to(message, address)` sets where replies go.
- `mail.header(message, name, value)` adds a header, such as
  `List-Unsubscribe`.
- `mail.tag(message, tag)` labels the message. Providers that support tags
  or categories receive them, and the outbox shows them.
- `mail.attach(message, attachment)` adds a file.

Functions that take a list add to what was there, so
`mail.to([a]) |> mail.to([b])` sends to both.

### Attachments

```gleam
let invoice =
  mail.attachment(
    filename: "invoice.pdf",
    content_type: "application/pdf",
    content: pdf,
  )

let logo =
  mail.attachment(filename: "logo.png", content_type: "image/png", content: png)
  |> mail.inline("logo")

message
|> mail.attach(invoice)
|> mail.attach(logo)
```

`mail.inline` shows an image inside the HTML body instead of listing it as
a download. The HTML refers to it as `<img src="cid:logo">`.

## Sending

A `Mailer` holds an adapter and the sender used by messages that do not
name their own:

```gleam
import howdy/mail
import howdy/mail/smtp

pub fn mailer() -> mail.Mailer {
  let assert Ok(config) = smtp.from_env()
  mail.mailer(smtp.adapter(config))
  |> mail.default_from(mail.named("Acme", "hello@acme.test"))
}
```

Build the mailer once when the app starts, and pass it to the services that
send mail. Then send:

```gleam
import logging

case mail.send(mailer, welcome(user)) {
  Ok(_receipt) -> Nil
  Error(error) ->
    logging.log(logging.Warning, "welcome: " <> mail.error_to_string(error))
}
```

`send` waits for the provider to accept or refuse the message, so a refusal
comes back as an error rather than a bounce.

### Sending is synchronous

`send` runs in the calling process. It opens the SMTP connection or makes
the HTTP request, and returns when the provider answers or the adapter's
timeout passes. A request handler that sends mail is held for that long,
and there is no queue in the package: an `Unavailable` error is returned to
the caller, not tried again later. That is fine for a sign-in link, where
the person is waiting for it anyway, and for a local Mailpit.

Where the wait matters, or a temporary failure must not lose the message,
you have two choices:

- The SMTP adapter can try again itself: `smtp.retries(config, 2)` retries
  a temporary failure twice, waiting `smtp.backoff` (500 ms by default,
  doubling each time) in between. The caller is held for every attempt,
  so this suits a job or a background process better than a request.
- Send it from a [background job](/docs/jobs/overview), queued in the
  same transaction as the change the email is about, and use
  `mail.retryable(error)` to decide what to try again. See
  [Queued email](/docs/jobs/overview#Queued-email). The
  [outbox](/docs/mail/development) is a sink for reading mail in
  development and tests, not that queue.

The `Receipt` holds the message's own `id`, which is also in its
`Message-ID` header, and the `provider_id` the provider gave it, if any.

### Checks

Before any adapter sees a message, `send` checks that it has:

- a sender, its own or the mailer's default
- at least one recipient
- a subject
- a body: HTML, text or both
- addresses that are addresses
- no line break in the subject, a display name, a header or a tag

The last check means text from a form can go into a subject or a name
without letting anyone add headers. `header` also refuses the headers the
package writes itself, such as `From`, `To`, `Subject`, `Date` and
`Content-Type`: use their own functions instead.

A message that fails is `Error(mail.Invalid(reason))`, and nothing is sent.

`mail.prepare(mailer, message)` runs the same checks and applies the
mailer's defaults, without sending. It returns the `mail.Outgoing` an
adapter would receive, which is how previews show a message as it would go
out.

### Errors

- `Invalid`: the message is wrong, such as a missing sender, recipient,
  subject or body, or a bad address or header. Sending it again will not
  help.
- `Unavailable`: the provider could not be reached or asked to be tried
  later, such as a timeout, an SMTP `4xx`, or an HTTP `429` or `5xx`. Worth
  retrying.
- `Refused`: the provider refused it for good, such as bad credentials, an
  unverified sender, or an SMTP `5xx`. Do not retry.

`mail.retryable(error)` says whether trying again might work.
`mail.error_to_string(error)` describes the error for your logs.

In a [service](/docs/controllers/services), turn the error into a service
error with `mail.to_service_error`:

```gleam
import gleam/result
import howdy/mail
import howdy/service

pub fn invite(mailer, email: String) -> service.Result(Nil) {
  use address <- result.try(
    mail.parse_address(email)
    |> result.replace_error(service.Invalid("not an email address")),
  )
  invitation(address)
  |> mail.send(mailer, _)
  |> result.map_error(mail.to_service_error)
  |> result.replace(Nil)
}
```

`Invalid` becomes `service.Invalid`, a `400` that explains itself.
`Unavailable` and `Refused` become `service.Internal`, which is logged but
not shown, so provider details never reach the client.
